From 03e0d3aa6bf0cb5681ee1655f8a886b08ced53bc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pokeheartgold-3ds <bootstrap@invalid>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 08:21:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] sound: do not write the finished-command tag before the
 shared work exists

The ARM7 driver learns the shared-work pointer from the command that carries
it (sub/lib/src/sound_command.c, the SND_CMD_INIT case: SNDi_SharedWork =
cmd.arg[0]). At the end of every processed command it then bumps
finishedCommandTag through that pointer. On the DS the ARM9 always sends the
setup command first, so the pointer is set; the vendored test_arm7's test_sound
calls SND_Init and the command APIs directly, and the port's virtual ARM7 can
process a queued command first, so the bump dereferenced NULL and the test
segfaulted.

Under SDK_PORT_3DS the bump is skipped while the pointer is NULL. That only
delays the counter the ARM9 polls; every status field is still updated by the
commands themselves. The Metrowerks build keeps the original line: gcc-10
-E -P without the macro is byte-identical.

Checked by vendored test_arm7 test_sound, which segfaulted before this patch.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 sub/lib/src/sound_command.c | 11 ++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/sub/lib/src/sound_command.c b/sub/lib/src/sound_command.c
index af710b2..30d4452 100644
--- a/sub/lib/src/sound_command.c
+++ b/sub/lib/src/sound_command.c
@@ -295,7 +295,16 @@ void SND_CommandProc(void) {
             list = cmd.llNext;
         }
 #ifdef SDK_PORT_3DS
-        (void)__atomic_add_fetch(&SNDi_SharedWork->finishedCommandTag, 1, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
+        /* The ARM7 only learns the shared-work pointer from the command that
+         * carries it (this file, SND_CMD_INIT below), which the DS sends
+         * before any other request. The port's virtual ARM7 can have a
+         * command queued first (the vendored test calls SND_Init and then
+         * the command APIs without a full ARM9 boot), and dereferencing NULL
+         * here crashed. The tag only tells the ARM9 how far the queue has
+         * got, so skipping it until the pointer exists is safe. */
+        if (SNDi_SharedWork != NULL) {
+            (void)__atomic_add_fetch(&SNDi_SharedWork->finishedCommandTag, 1, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
+        }
 #else
         SNDi_SharedWork->finishedCommandTag++;
 #endif
